Slide 1 — Cover (30 sec)
"Hi everyone, we're presenting Safe Login: a machine learning project that detects suspicious logins. I'm [your name], and I worked on this with Pranav Das, Julia Jeng, and Aarush Kikkuru. This is a Python-based cybersecurity project built around a Random Forest classifier."
Slide 2 — Introduction (55 sec)
"So why does this matter? A correct password doesn't actually prove that the person logging in is the real account owner — passwords get leaked, guessed, or stolen all the time. But the login itself carries a lot of hidden context: the time of day, the location, the device, the network. Individually these are weak signals, but a machine learning model can combine all of them together and make a risk decision. That led us to our core project question: can a machine learning model use login context to identify suspicious activity?"
Slide 3 — Design of the system (50 sec)
"Here's how our system is structured at a high level. On the input side, we feed in things like login time, location and network information, and device details, plus login success and round-trip time. That goes into our model — a Random Forest, which combines many decision trees to classify each login. And on the output side, the system doesn't just give a probability — it applies a tuned decision threshold and returns a final label: either normal/low risk, or suspicious/malicious."
Slide 4 — Implementation (60 sec)
"For implementation, we used the Risk-Based Authentication dataset from Zenodo — over 33 million login attempts across 3.3 million users, about 1.1 gigabytes compressed. Our pipeline reads that data in chunks so we're not loading everything into memory at once, cleans and engineers features like login hour and day of week, balances the classes, splits the data into train, validation, and test sets, trains the model, and tunes and saves it. For the model itself, we used scikit-learn's Random Forest Classifier with 250 decision trees, balanced class weights so the rare attack class gets more influence during training, a 60/20/20 data split, and a threshold tuned on validation F1 score rather than just assuming 0.5."
Slide 5 — Results (55 sec)
"Here's how the model actually performed on our test set. The metric we cared about most was recall — 87.33%, meaning the model caught nearly 9 out of every 10 attack-IP logins. Accuracy came in at 85.6%, and ROC-AUC, which measures how well the model separates the two classes, was strong at 92.2%. Precision was more moderate at 54%, which you can see in the confusion matrix — we correctly caught over 17,000 attack logins, but also flagged about 14,700 normal logins as suspicious. So the model favors catching attacks, even at the cost of some false alarms."
Slide 6 — Problem encountered (55 sec)
"One of the biggest challenges we ran into was picking the right thing to predict. We originally tried to predict 'Is Account Takeover' — but in our first million rows, there were only 11 positive examples. That's nowhere near enough data, and the model basically learned to just predict 'normal' for everything, which gave a misleadingly high accuracy score. So we switched our main target to 'Is Attack IP' instead, which had over 99,000 positive examples in that same sample — enough to actually train, validate, and test the model properly. The big lesson here: a high accuracy number can be meaningless if the thing you're trying to detect barely shows up in your data."
Slide 7 — Future enhancements (45 sec)
"Looking ahead, there's a clear path to make this more production-ready. We'd want to test on a natural, unbalanced sample of the data to get realistic false-alarm rates, use time-based splitting so we're training on earlier logins and testing on later ones, compare against other models like XGBoost or gradient boosting, add explainability tools like SHAP, and eventually build a simple dashboard where someone could enter login details and see a live risk score."
Slide 8 — Who did what (35 sec)
"As a team, [briefly describe Pranav, Julia, and Aarush's parts here based on what they actually did]. On my end, I built the full ML training and prediction pipeline, diagnosed and fixed the rare-label problem by switching our prediction target, fixed several bugs including data leakage, ASN encoding, and timestamp handling, added feature importance and more realistic accuracy testing, and put together the README and this presentation."
Slide 9 — Conclusion (35 sec)
"To wrap up: we built a complete, end-to-end machine learning prototype that successfully detects most attack-IP logins by combining contextual signals, with 87% recall and a full Python pipeline from data download all the way to prediction. And we tried to be upfront about its limitations — it's a strong prototype, but not production-ready yet. Thanks for listening, happy to take any questions."
